# @VERSION@ - what changed

The install guide sits beside this file, in `INSTALL.md`. This one is
only the history.

It goes all the way back to the first published build, so you never have
to open a browser to find out what changed between the version you had
and the version you have.


## This build - @VERSION@

@CHANGES@


## What it does

- **Updates** - as the game starts it says *"Checking for updates"*,
  then whether you have the latest version, then *"Main menu"*; a newer
  version is offered in a popup that talks:
  download, checked against its checksum, and put in place when you
  quit. *Options*, *Accessibility*, *Check for updates* asks by hand.
- **Menus** - moving to an item speaks it together with what it is set
  to, changing it speaks the new value, and confirmation dialogs are
  read out.
- **Enter changes the setting you are on.** Every settings control in
  the game wanted the mouse pointer inside it as well as keyboard
  focus, and arrowing onto one does not move the pointer - so Enter did
  nothing, on every checkbox, every yes/no and every game type. A
  tester found it unable to choose a game type while hosting a server.
  Clicking is unchanged.
- **Sliders step with the left and right arrow keys**, speaking the new
  value each time - effects volume, music volume, mouse sensitivity,
  and the forty-odd others. Around twenty presses end to end, on a
  ladder of round numbers rather than a flat twentieth of each range.
  Pressing past an end says the value again rather than going quiet.
- **The controls list can be read and changed.** Each row says its name
  and the key it is on - *"Crouch, C"* - and Enter starts a capture
  that tells you it is listening: *"press the key to use. Escape to
  cancel, backspace to clear."* The next key you press is taken and
  read back. Until now the list spoke only the control's name, which
  made it look as though nothing could be changed at all.
- **Text boxes** - arriving at one reads what it holds, every character
  you type is echoed, and the arrow keys read it back one character at a
  time. Passwords are spoken as a length, never read out.
- **Team, class and weapon** - the limbo panel, which is mouse-only in
  the stock game, gets keyboard navigation and speaks each team and
  class along with whether it is full, unavailable, or the one you are
  on. Each weapon slot is a stop too, spoken as "Primary weapon,
  Thompson, 1 of 3" with the game's own count for your class and skill
  level.
- **Health and ammo keys** - one each, under the hand that is already on
  the movement keys, because one long report is the wrong shape for "am
  I about to die".
- **Stance** - crouching, standing up and going prone are spoken as they
  happen. If the game refuses to let you stand because something is over
  your head, you hear that you are still crouched rather than being left
  to guess.
- **Status key** - everything at once: health, weapon, ammo, the current
  objective, how far away it is in metres, and whether to turn left,
  turn right, or carry straight on.
- **The marker's distance** counts itself down as you close in - 100,
  50, 25 and 10 metres, with up or down - so you can hear that you are
  getting there. Keypad plus puts the marker on the objective. The old
  *"meters to objective"*, counted in a straight line, is gone. The
  objective comes from the same list your command map draws, so it
  works on every map, not only the ones that name a main objective.
- **Furniture and things in a room are said** as you come within a
  metre and a half: a chair, a bed, a bookcase, a crate, a window from
  inside - read from the names the map gives them.
- **Pickups are heard where they lie** - health packs, ammo packs and
  weapons within ten metres and in sight, each its own sound; a dropped
  objective rings the flag's bell.
- **Health and ammo cabinets are heard** - the health pack's and the
  ammo pack's sounds, from the cabinet, when it is in sight within ten
  metres.
- **SAPI 5 lets each line finish** - with NVDA closed, a new line used
  to cut off the one before it, so most of what was said was lost.
- **The tank rumbles** - the one to escort, like the Jagdpanther on
  Goldrush; damaged, it taps for the side that repairs it.
- **F keeps you facing what you chose** - walking to it the view
  follows it, and *"in reach, F to use"* says when F will pick it up.
- **Walking into something is heard** - a thud for a wall, a knock for
  a thing.
- **Landmines are warned of** - *"Enemy landmine, ahead, 6 meters"*,
  with fast beeps from it close by, and the marker's way steps round
  them; only the mines the game shows you, spotted by your team or
  seen with the skill for traps.
- **How you died is said** in the game's words - *"You failed to spot
  Hans's Landmine"* - and your kills.
- **Who you watch is said** - *"following Hans, Axis Medic, 85
  health"*, then their kills, their death and *"Hans revived"*.
- **The console says when it opens** - *"Console open. The same key
  closes it"*, and *"Console closed"*.
- **The way goes through low tunnels crouched** - *"Crouch, low ceiling
  ahead"*, with the key, and *"Stand up"* once out from under.
- **The way out of a building on ;** - the marker goes on the nearest
  way out under the open sky and leads you through the door; again for
  another door. Outside, on the nearest way into a building.
- **Accessibility → Sound**: a volume for each of the mod's sounds -
  the marker, objective sounds, doors, ladders, the crosshair, the
  alerts - and for the game's announcer, each from 0 to 100.
- **The objectives key** reads the map's objective text for your team as
  well as showing the panel, with no rebinding.
- **Getting wedged** reads out the objective, its distance and which way
  to turn - only when you are pressing a movement key and getting
  nowhere.
- **What is blocking you** is named when something actually is - a wall,
  a door - found by sweeping your own body's shape in the direction you
  are *asking* to move, and only while you are asking to move and not
  getting anywhere. Stairs pass underneath it and the walls beside a
  corridor stay quiet while you run past them.
- **Objectives in the map's own order.** ET's maps run their objectives
  in sequence, and the mod used to point at whichever was nearest -
  usually one that cannot be started yet. The status and objectives keys
  now open with *"Objective 2 of 4"* and the map's description of it,
  finishing one is announced as it happens, and the navigation walks you
  to the one you can actually do.
- **Options - Accessibility** opens a short menu of four pages.
  **Environment**: fourteen yes/no rows for what the mod tells you about
  the world. **Speech**: four sliders for the voice and the music. **Key
  Binds**: twenty-five bind rows, each saying which key it is on and
  changeable from the keyboard - the mod's own twenty-one, plus smooth
  turn left, smooth turn right, look up and look down, which are the
  *game's* commands and which the game's own controls list offers
  nowhere at all. The game's CONTROLS page is left as the game ships it.
  Resetting the controls to default no longer takes the mod's keys away,
  which it used to do permanently.
  **Hosting**: whether friends join a game you host with the plain game,
  and how many Omni-bot bots play - changed during the match, at once.
- **A tap on Left or Right turns to the next compass point** and says
  it as it lands, the way a flick of the mouse does; two taps turn a
  quarter. Holding still turns smoothly, for aiming, and names each
  point as you cross it.
- **The marker's ping never points into a wall.** When a wall is in the
  way it moves to the nearest open way out, and behind that the mod
  works out a whole way you can walk - through the doorway, round the
  corner - that the ping and "turn left or right" follow.
- **The loading screen is said** - connecting, downloading, the map's
  name and briefing, the loading bar by the quarter, and *"Loaded. Press
  L to choose your team and class"* when it is over.
- **The build is counted out while you aim at it.** The game hides the
  progress from a player looking at their own construction; the mod now
  reads it from the construction itself, so the percentages and
  *"Construction complete"* are heard wherever you look.
- **The map key opens and closes the map.** One press opens it -
  *"Map open"* - and it stays open while you walk the survey; the next
  press closes it. Environment - *Map key opens and closes* turns it
  back into a key to hold.
- **Objectives are read without their stars.** The game breaks its
  objective text into lines with a star; each is now a sentence break.
- **Menu labels are read as words.** The game hides a hotkey marker
  inside button names - Play is stored as *PL^_AY* - and colour codes
  inside player and server names. Both are taken out before anything is
  spoken, so nothing is spelled out.
- **F9 says what is around you** - indoors or out, which ways are open
  and how far by compass point, the walls, a window, what blocks a way
  when it is a thing, and the named things close by - a truck, a crate,
  a health cabinet - with direction, distance and whether they are in
  sight.
- **Players near you are said as they come and go** - an enemy in
  clear sight, a wounded teammate, teammates close by: ahead, left,
  right or behind, how far, and again as they come closer or move away.
  Never an enemy through a wall.
- **Things on the ground are said as you reach them** - a weapon, ammo,
  a health pack; and for a covert op, an enemy body with its uniform.
- **A covert op takes a uniform with F** - F faces the enemy body and
  holds use until the uniform is on. An enemy just shot down is listed
  too: their body, and its uniform, come once they give up - don't
  shoot them again.
- **No more "Object"** - what the narrator meets is said as what it is:
  a mounted gun, a cabinet, a landmine, a body, a construction with its
  objective; a nameless part of the map as the wall or ground it is.
- **Rooms** - ' puts the marker on the nearest room you can walk into,
  with its name, floor and size; again for the next. Each square of the
  map survey says how many rooms it has.
- **Stairs on the way, and on F** - *"Stairs up, ahead left, 3 meters"*
  before a flight on the way to the marker; jumps name the jump key. F
  turns to stairs close by, and at their foot straight up or down them.
- **Sniper spots** - / puts the marker on the nearest high place that
  looks out a long way - a rooftop, a balcony, a ledge, an upper floor
  window - and says what it sees; again for the next. Found in the map's
  own walls and floors, and only those with a way up.
- **The kind of place** - a street and which way it runs, an alley, a
  square, a building and its upper floor, a balcony, a roof, a ledge with
  its drop - said as you come into it, worked out from the map's own
  walls and floors, on F9, and for each square of the map survey.
- **Choosing what to use** - ] and [ step through the things to use
  close by, said without turning you; F then turns to the one chosen and
  uses it in reach, whatever the crosshair is on.
- **Aiming, with a key and by itself** - F with nothing under the
  crosshair turns to the nearest thing to use - using it in reach,
  naming it further off, the next one on another press; and the enemy
  lock
  keeps your sights on an enemy in sight - by itself, or while INS is
  held - in a game you host or where the server allows it.
- **The map has the keys while it is open** - the arrows walk the map,
  and nothing moves until it is closed.
- **Openings are said as you walk** - *"Opening to the east"* at a side
  street or a corner, *"Wall to the east"* when it closes again - so you
  hear where a street begins and ends. Environment - *Openings as you
  walk* turns it off.
- **The scoreboard, player stats and top shots speak** - the time left,
  both teams, where you stand and every player's XP; your kills,
  accuracy, damage, rank and weapons; the best player with each weapon.
- **Every door creaks**, including the team doors the server never
  marks, and a door your team cannot open creaks as locked.
- **Compass zoom speaks** - which of the fourteen steps it is on and how
  many metres across the compass shows.
- **Look up and look down work in a match.** The game deleted both
  commands every time a match loaded, so the arrow keys did nothing in
  play.
- **A marker of your own.** Open the map, walk the cursor to a
  square and press Keypad 0 - or press it with the map down to mark
  where you stand. A ping along the way there and distance callouts
  walk you there - a high ping in front, a lower one to either side and
  a low double ping behind - and *"Marker reached"* says you have arrived.
  Keypad dot asks where it is, Keypad star clears it.
- **The way to the objective, up as well as round.** Keypad plus puts
  the marker on the objective - press it again for the next one on your
  command map - and the ping leads you there by stairs,
  ladders and ledges - *"Jump up, ahead"* at a ledge, F to climb at a
  ladder - instead of into the wall under an objective on a floor above.
- **The music gets out of the narrator's way.** A tester turned the
  music up and found the narrator underneath it - which no volume
  setting could have fixed, because the music is mixed by the game and
  the speech goes out through Windows, and neither can see the other.
  The mod now dips the music while the voice is on it and brings it
  straight back, using the game's own music fade. Off by default;
  `eta_musicDuck 0.3` turns it on. A map that fades its own music keeps
  its level - the dip is a fraction of what the map asked for.
- **The narrator's voice can be shaped on SAPI 5** - rate, pitch and
  volume. Not on NVDA, and deliberately so: NVDA speaks at the settings
  its own user chose, and a game overriding them would be a bug. If you
  are on NVDA, the music ducking above is the setting that helps.
- **Settings take effect when you change them.** They used not to: every
  setting the mod adds was read once at map load and never re-read, so
  changing one stored the value and changed nothing until the next map,
  with nothing anywhere to say so.
- **The arrow keys turn and look.** Stock ET binds no key to either -
  aiming is the mouse and nothing but the mouse, so the compass could
  tell you that you were facing south and leave you no way to act on
  it. Left and right turn on the spot; up and down tip the view, and
  what you end up pointing at is spoken: *"Open sky"*, *"Concrete
  ground, 3 meters"*, *"Wooden ceiling"*. It is the same trace the
  binoculars use, so it never reports through a wall, and it waits for
  the aim to settle rather than narrating the sweep.
- **The command map, square by square.** Press the map key to open the
  map - it stays open until the next press - and the keypad's arrow
  keys walk a cursor across the map's own lettered grid,
  saying what is in each square and how far away and in which direction
  it lies -
  *"C,3. Axis command post, two teammates. 40 metres, north-east, 52
  degrees."* The squares are the ones your team reads off their screens.
  It reports what the game sends your team and nothing more.
- **The scenery waits when you have asked a question.** Speech has one
  voice, so a report arriving mid-answer destroys it. Pressing a status
  or objectives key keeps the scenery quiet for a few seconds. The drop
  warning is the one exception, and deliberately so.
- **Stairs** are called ahead of you, up or down, and a single step is
  told from a flight of them. The ground is measured rather than looked
  at, and a ramp is left unsaid rather than called stairs.
- **What you can use** is named when the game shows the icon for it -
  *"Pliers, build here"*, *"Pliers, defuse here"*, *"Pliers, arm
  dynamite here"* over your team's own dynamite, *"Dynamite goes
  here"*, *"Revive"*, *"Health pack"* - with the progress figure where
  the game draws one beside the icon. The interaction sound still fires
  the instant the icon appears; the words follow a quarter of a second
  behind, so turning through a supply room does not read out everything
  you swept past.
- **The build is counted out** while you hold the pliers on a
  construction - *"Building, 27 percent"*, *"Building, 52 percent"*,
  *"Building, 78 percent"*, *"Building, 91 percent"* - and finishes
  with *"Construction complete"*. Four figures on the way up rather
  than continuously, and it counts out again from where a part-built
  construction actually stands when you come back to it. The
  completion is the game's own event, the one that fills the bar to
  the top on screen; the figure itself never reaches 100, because the
  game resets it in the same frame the work finishes. Every
  constructible, on every map.
- **Binoculars and scopes** say which one is up, and what your view runs
  into - a person and whose side they are on, a door, or the wall,
  ground or ceiling in front of you and what it is made of. It keeps
  talking as you sweep across a scene.
- **Drops** - when the ground in front of you falls away, you are told
  so and how far down it goes, and whether the fall would hurt or kill
  you. Those two bands are the game's own falling-damage thresholds
  worked back into heights, using the gravity actually in play, rather
  than a guess about what counts as high. It looks where you are going
  when you are moving and where you are facing when you are standing
  still.
- **Water** - wading in, getting deep enough that a jump becomes a
  swim, going under, and coming back out. Those are the engine's own
  waterlevels, which is why "deep water" and "swimming" mean something
  specific: they are the points at which your own movement changes.
- **Doors creak.** Every door within about ten metres has a creaking
  sound placed on it, positioned in the world, so it swings around you
  as you turn and centres when you face it. A locked door creaks
  differently - duller and slower - so you can hear that it will not
  open without walking to it. Doors are identified by the game's own
  classification, not guessed from the entity, so lifts and tank
  barriers are not mistaken for them.
- **Construction sites tap, demolition targets hiss, and flags ring.**
  A site your team can build taps like a hammer on steel; an enemy
  objective you can blow up - a dynamite target, or an enemy
  construction that can be destroyed - hisses like a lit fuse; a flag
  you can capture rings a two-note bell. Within about twenty-five
  metres, positioned in the world like the doors, and each falls silent
  once it is built, destroyed or yours.
- **Spectating or in play is said** as it changes - *"Spectating,
  following"* a name, *"In play, Allies, Medic"*, *"Down, a medic can
  revive you"*, *"Waiting to respawn"* - and the status key opens with
  it.
- **Ladders are said, and F climbs them** - *"Ladder down, ahead left,
  2 meters. F climbs"*; F turns you onto it and climbs up or down to the
  end, stepping off the edge onto a ladder below the way the game needs.
- **The map survey names flags and counts doors and ladders** in each
  square - the flags to capture, the doors that are the ways into
  buildings, and the ladders up and down inside them.
- **Thrown dynamite is found again.** When your dynamite stops rolling
  the narrator says where - *"Dynamite down, 2 meters ahead left, not
  armed"* - and for an engineer F turns to dynamite in sight: your
  team's to arm, the enemy's armed one to defuse.
- **Ladders knock.** Every ladder within about fifteen metres, the
  nearest two at a time, plays three knocks on wooden rungs from the
  ladder itself, and F turns you to one in sight like anything else to
  use. Ladders are part of the map's walls, not anything the
  game sends, so they are read out of the map file as it loads.
- **Up or down is said** - *"Command post, ahead left, 12 meters, 4
  meters above you"* - for the objective, the distances called on the
  way to it, and whatever F turns to, whenever it is two metres or more
  off your own level.
- **The crosshair ticks when it is on somebody** - bright for an enemy,
  low and soft for a teammate, fast enough that a quick sweep across a
  target still registers. It reads the game's own crosshair, so it never
  sees through a wall, and a disguised covert operative sounds like a
  teammate, because that is exactly what a sighted player sees.
- **Picking anything up** is spoken - health, ammo, a weapon off the
  ground, an objective - in the game's own words, with how much it gave
  you and what you now have. A pack you were too full to use says
  "already full" rather than sounding like one that helped.
- **Where you are** - the map's own name for the place, *"Old City
  Wall"*, *"Axis Command Post"*, taken from the same list that fills in
  the location when a sighted player types in team chat. So the words
  mean something to your team. Maps that ship no list fall back to the
  command map's grid square, so there is always an answer. It waits
  until you have stopped somewhere rather than naming everywhere you
  walk through, and the status key reports it on demand, along with
  whether you have open sky overhead - which is what an airstrike or a
  mortar round needs to reach you.
- **Which way you are facing** is announced when it changes, and waits
  for you to stop turning first - a sweep of the mouse says where you
  ended up rather than reading out every heading it passed through.
- **Changing weapon** says the weapon, its loaded rounds and what is
  left in reserve, however the change happened. Every weapon a class can
  hold has a name, including the ones the game itself leaves blank.
- **Repeat and stop** - one key says the last line again, another cuts
  speech off rather than making you wait out a long report.
- **Cues** - low health, objective change, something you can interact
  with, the direction damage came from, and the direction of the
  objective.

The cues are the game's own sounds played through the engine, so they
follow your volume settings and the two directional ones are positioned
in the world like any other sound.


## What has been heard, and what has not

Everything in this mod compiles, links and passes an automated suite on
every change, and the parts where being wrong would be worse than being
silent - which way to turn, which compass point you are facing, how many
metres away something is, which surface you are standing on - are
checked against Wolfenstein: Enemy Territory's own code rather than
against the mod's idea of it.

None of that can tell you whether a cue fires at the right moment,
whether the speech is too fast or too frequent to follow, whether it
talks over your screen reader, or whether a direction means anything
useful while you are actually moving. Those need ears, and the list of
what has had them is below.

`INSTALL.md` ends with the specific things worth reporting back.

### What is checked, and what is not

Confirmed working with a real screen reader: the menus, the team and
class panel, text boxes, navigation assistance, the stance narration,
the named class weapons, the objectives key, the weapon reports, the
binoculars, the weapon scope, the door creak, the crosshair tick and
the location narration.

**The command map survey works.** It was played through a full match
after the key installer was fixed: holding the map key and walking the
grid gave a starting point, a route to the first
objective, and the pump on Oasis - which was then built. That is the
whole point of the feature, tested end to end by someone who cannot see
the map.

**The objectives are announced in order.** The same session reported
the narrator naming the primary objective and working down the list,
which is what the ordering was written for.

**The interaction icons work.** The pliers icon announced itself on the
construction in that match, which is how the player knew there was
something there to build at all.

**The locations work.** *"I reached the enemy camp"*, *"I found the pump
on Oasis, and I did not know that until the narrator told me."*

Confirmed working by a tester in this release: the Accessibility
settings page, and the music stepping back for the narrator.

Confirmed in play by the tester of beta-0.00.9: tap turning, the
compass zoom, the marker and the three-page Accessibility menu.

Confirmed in play by the tester of beta-0.01.0: the scoreboard, the
player stats and the top shots.

Confirmed in play by the tester of beta-0.01.2: F9, the openings as
you walk, and the menu labels.

Confirmed in play by the tester of beta-0.01.5: the build progress
while aiming at the construction.

Reported by the tester of beta-0.01.5: the marker's ping still led into
the wall. That is what this build fixes, tested by walking a player to
the marker by the ping in a simulation; it has not been heard in play.

Not yet heard by anyone in play: the marker's ping keeping off the walls
and the loading screen's speech, which are new in this build; the map
key as a switch
and the objectives without their stars, new in beta-0.01.3; the
team-door creak; changing the accessibility keys from the Key Binds page, which
could not be done until now; the compass naming each point as a held key
crosses it; and the up-and-down report, which could not be heard in a
match until the look keys were fixed; the drop warning and the water narration; the objective distance
countdown; the amounts reported on a pickup; the stairs report; the
scenery hold; and the ground underfoot and the lean announcements.

The wall report has been heard and corrected three times. The third
correction - aiming the probe where the player is going rather than
where they are looking, and speaking only while they are actually
stopped - has now been through two full test sessions without being
reported against, which is not the same as somebody confirming it and
is the best evidence there is.

The compass was heard and reported as unable to keep up with the mouse.
Not speaking until the view stops turning was the fix; it has been in
play since without a further report.

What CI proves on every change, against engine checkouts cloned fresh
and patched from scratch each run. The first nine are checked on both
supported trees - ET: Legacy, which is what this download is built
from, and the 2010 GPL release, which is not shipped here. The last
three apply to ET: Legacy alone, because the weapon table, the menus and
the shipped config are what this package installs:

- **The directions are not mirrored.** The real bearing expressions are
  lifted out of the patched engine and compiled against that engine's
  own maths, then given a target on the player's right. This project
  told players to turn left when the objective was on their right, in
  both integrations, and shipped it.
- **The runtime is actually started.** Every library subsystem the
  engine reaches must have its Init reached too, followed through the
  library's own call graph. Narration wired to a backend nobody
  initialised is silent, compiles cleanly, and is what made alpha-0.1.0
  speechless.
- **The keys cannot hang the game.** The repeat and stop commands hand
  menu requests between the two mod libraries; if that handover ever
  named a command both answer, it would loop inside the engine with no
  output at all.
- **The navigation can find an objective.** The game's own list of
  objective types is read out of the engine, and the build fails if the
  narration cannot place one of them, or if anything announces a
  distance or a direction it did not get from the single lookup that
  chooses the target. Before this, the target came from a map script
  command most maps never run, so the whole feature was silent on most
  maps and no check here could see it.
- **The stance is the right one.** Going prone sets the game's crouch
  flag as well, so narration that checks crouch first would call every
  prone player crouched and never say "prone". The build reads that
  overlap out of the game's own movement code and fails if the check is
  written the wrong way round.
- **The metres are real metres.** Distances are spoken in metres, and
  the game measures in its own units with no stated conversion. The
  game does state the size of a person, though, in five separate places
  - so the build checks that those five come out human-sized. They do:
  1.83 m standing, 1.63 m to the eye, 1.22 m crouched. A conversion
  wrong by even a fifth fails all five at once.
- **The look-ahead reports what the game sees.** "What is in front of
  me" is answered by the game's own collision, not a guess, and the
  materials are the flags it uses for footstep sounds. The build fails
  if either stops being true.
- **Every icon the game draws has words.** The list of interaction
  icons is read out of the engine's own drawing code, and the build
  fails if the mod has nothing to say for one of them - silence there
  is indistinguishable from there being nothing to use. The same check
  fails on a mapping written by icon number rather than by name: the
  two supported trees number these differently and both leave gaps, so
  a number that is right in one announces the wrong thing in the other.
- **Nothing picked up goes unspoken.** The narration hangs on the one
  place both pickup paths funnel through, and speaks the game's own
  on-screen message; the build fails if either stops being true.
- **Every weapon has something to say.** The game leaves fifteen of its
  weapons unnamed, which is why grenades once announced their ammo and
  nothing else. The build walks every weapon the number keys can reach
  and fails if any would be announced nameless.
- **Typed characters are echoed correctly.** The character extractor is
  lifted out of the patched engine and compiled against the engine's own
  UTF-8 code, then asked for characters whose answers are known. An echo
  off by one, or half a multi-byte character handed to a screen reader
  to pronounce, compiles perfectly and is wrong only to the person
  listening.
- **The keys name things that exist** (ET: Legacy only). Every bind and
  setting in `autoexec.cfg`, *and* every bind the mod installs for
  itself, is checked against the engine's real command tables. A
  misspelled command binds successfully and does nothing; a misspelled
  setting is stored and never read. The mod's own binds are worse than
  the config's, because there is no file to inspect when they fail.

Each of those exists because of a defect that reached a release or would
have. Each fails loudly when it finds nothing to check, because a test
that quietly tests nothing is how every one of them got through.

What none of it can tell you: whether a cue fires at the right moment,
whether the speech is too fast or too frequent to follow, whether it
talks over your screen reader, or whether "turn right" means anything
useful while you are actually moving. Those need ears.

`INSTALL.md` ends with the specific things worth reporting.


@HISTORY@
